  root-len                  unknown length-delimited field at the root
  elem-varint-interleaved   unknown varint INSIDE element 0, at tag 7 -- the gap ResultRaw leaves between `completed_at` (6) and `result_id` (8), so it sits numerically BETWEEN two known tags
  elem-i64                  unknown fixed64 inside element 0
  elem-i32                  unknown fixed32 inside element 0
  elem-len-nested           unknown length-delimited field inside element 0 whose body is itself a message -- a decoder that recursed into it rather than skipping it would report the inner tags as unknown fields of the OUTER message
  oneof-unrecognised-member an unrecognised ONEOF member: tag 15 sits one past `as_nothing` (14) in Probe.body. design/SHAPES.md's row says this CANNOT round trip in any arm, because the grouping lives only in the descriptor and a parser cannot tell it from any other unknown field

Reading the two retention columns.
  `YES` means the arm's re-encode differs from the payload with the unknown
  field removed, which for these vectors means it wrote the unknown field back.

  Google.Protobuf keeps an UnknownFieldSet per message and writes it back, so a
  message round-tripped through .NET today preserves a field this build has never
  heard of. The managed codec's DEFAULT reader drops it; with Dec.Retain set it keeps
  it and writes it back (last column: required byte-identical to Google.Protobuf). Both accept
  the input and neither loses a KNOWN value, so this is not a correctness gate;
  it is ABI v1 open decision 11 with a cost attached, and on .NET the cost is a
  guarantee that exists today and would be removed. prost drops unknown fields
  too, which is why the Rust slice could not price this.

  The oneof vector is the one design/SHAPES.md says cannot round trip in ANY arm.
  Google.Protobuf retains the BYTES of tag 15 but the case stays at whatever
  member the element already carried; the grouping is descriptor-only, so no
  parser can recover the intent. Retention makes the bytes survive and does not
  make the VALUE survive, and those are different claims.

